{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Fall Martin ("Case Martin") was a plan created by Field Marshal Gerd von Rundstedt in late 1944 during World War II.","title":"Fall Martin"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The plan envisaged a two-pronged attack aimed at the Allied forces in eastern Belgium and Luxembourg.","title":"Fall Martin"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Walter Karl Ernst August von Reichenau (8 October 1884 – 17 January 1942) was a field marshal in the Wehrmacht of Nazi Germany during World War II.","title":"Walther von Reichenau"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A professional solider and veteran of World War I and the Reichswehr, he was one of twelve officers promoted at the 1940 Field Marshal Ceremony, and the most junior.","title":"Walther von Reichenau"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" An avid sportsman and athlete he was a member of the International Olympic Committee.","title":"Walther von Reichenau"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He is widely cited for his support of genocidal policies on the Eastern front and for his Severity Order, which he issued while in command of the German 6th Army.","title":"Walther von Reichenau"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Wilhelm Keitel (22 September 1882 – 16 October 1946) was a German field marshal who served as Chief of the Armed Forces High Command ("Oberkommando der Wehrmacht" or OKW) in Nazi Germany during World War II.","title":"Wilhelm Keitel"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" According to David Stahel, Keitel was "well known and [...] reviled as Hitler's dependable mouthpiece and habitual yes-man" among his military colleagues.","title":"Wilhelm Keitel"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Karl Rudolf Gerd von Rundstedt (12 December 1875 – 24 February 1953) was a Field Marshal in the Wehrmacht of Nazi Germany during World War II.","title":"Gerd von Rundstedt"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Kurt Wilhelm Albert Karl Agricola (15 August 1889 – 27 December 1955) was a general in the Wehrmacht of Nazi Germany during World War II who held senior level occupational rear-security commands in the occupied Soviet Union.","title":"Kurt Agricola"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" A native of Saxony, Agricola entered army service in 1908 and served during World War I.","title":"Kurt Agricola"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" During the interwar era, he held staff assignments and continued to rise through the army's ranks in the Wehrmacht of Nazi Germany.","title":"Kurt Agricola"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" His career ended stalled in January 1939, when he was sent into retirement on political grounds because of his marriage to Martha born Hahn, a Jewish woman.","title":"Kurt Agricola"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Reactivated again upon the start of World War II, Agricola received exclusively positions behind the front line.","title":"Kurt Agricola"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" As rear area commander of the 2nd Army in the occupied Soviet union during 1941–43, Agricola brought changes in the Wehrmacht's harsh occupation policies and was successful in maintaining control of his area of occupied territory from Soviet partisans.","title":"Kurt Agricola"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Shortly after the war's end, he was arrested by Soviet authorities, convicted of war crimes and remained in captivity for a decade.","title":"Kurt Agricola"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" One of the last German prisoners in the Soviet Union, he was released in October 1955 and died shortly thereafter in West Germany.","title":"Kurt Agricola"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Bila Tserkva massacre was the World War II mass murder of Jews, committed by the Nazi German "Einsatzgruppe" with the aid of Ukrainian auxiliaries, in Bila Tserkva, Soviet Ukraine, on August 21–22, 1941.","title":"1941 Bila Tserkva massacre"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" When the Jewish adult population of Bila Tserkva was killed, several functionaries complained that some 90 Jewish children were left behind in an abandoned building, and had to be executed separately.","title":"1941 Bila Tserkva massacre"},{"sentence_id":